-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Precision:** The back-projection and interpolation steps require careful handling of floating-point precision to avoid artifacts in the feature volume [S1].
-*   **Boundaries:** The projection relies on valid camera parameters; invalid poses or intrinsics may lead to incorrect feature placement [S1].
-*   **Degenerate Input:** If the input image lacks sufficient texture or detail, the extracted features $F_I$ may be insufficient for accurate 3D reconstruction [S1].
+**Parameters**
+*   $D$: Spatial resolution of each plane. Not given in the sources.
+*   $C$: Feature dimension. Not given in the sources.
+*   $G$: Output channel dimension, defined as $G = C/4$ [S1].
+
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls**
+*   **Missing Features:** The method relies on 2D convolutions to act as inpainting networks to fill in missing features in the triplanes, suggesting that sparse input views may result in incomplete initial projections [S1].
+*   **Depth Ambiguity:** The explicit depth encoding MLP $Z$ is required because features along a ray are identical in the raw back-projected volume; without this, the triplane representation would lose depth information [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Avoids the cubic complexity of 3D CNNs and voxel-based representations [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Linear in the number of planes and their resolution, avoiding cubic scaling with volume size [S1].
-*   **Expressivity:** Full expressivity at scale, modeling all three dimensions unlike 2D or BEV representations [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Computationally efficient compared to 3D CNNs; more expressive than 2D/BEV methods; acts as a global feature representation [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Relies on the quality of the initial image features and camera parameters [S1].
-*   **When to Use:** Sparse view synthesis of outdoor scenes where computational efficiency and expressivity are critical [S1].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When high-resolution volumetric details are required that cannot be captured by planar projections [S1].
+*   **Complexity:** Avoids the cubic complexity of voxel-based representations ($O(D^3)$) by using a fixed number of 2D planes ($O(D^2)$ per plane, total $O(D^2)$) [S1].
+*   **Expressiveness:** More expressive than BEV or 2D feature representations because it retains all three dimensions (unlike BEV which omits the z-axis) [S1].
+*   **Performance:** Enables accurate neural rendering and full expressivity at scale [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Computationally efficient compared to 3D CNNs or voxels; leverages pre-trained 2D CNNs for feature extraction [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Relies on the quality of the input image features and camera poses; performance may degrade with very sparse views if the inpainting mechanism is insufficient [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The Image-Conditional Triplanar Representation was introduced in "NeO 360: Neural Fields for Sparse View Synthesis of Outdoor Scenes" [S1]. It builds on prior works involving volumetric reconstruction [S1]. The method is designed to address the limitations of voxel-based and 2D/BEV representations in modeling 3D scenes [S1].
+The Image-Conditional Triplanar Representation was introduced in the paper "NeO 360: Neural Fields for Sparse View Synthesis of Outdoor Scenes" [S1]. It builds upon prior works involving volumetric reconstruction [55, 39, 25] and aims to address the limitations of cubic complexity in voxels and the lack of expressiveness in 2D/BEV representations [S1].
 
